instruction says \"produce a JSON object following the layered schema\". The schema fields listed are what's expected. Production locations aren't applicants or manufacturers per se — but they are manufacturing sites. I'll include them under `manufacturers[]` since that's the closest match, and each has name + address.\n\n4. Revision history: revisions Initial through 14 with dates and descriptions.\n\nNow for the tables. The tables are configuration-dependent: bore type (reduced/full) × power supply (AC mains / battery-renewable). Meter size is the axis. So this is config_level with two axes: bore type and power supply. Or config_level entries with axis like \"bore_and_power_supply\".\n\nPer rule 5: \"When attributes vary by configuration (not by model — e.g. 2-sensor-row vs 4-sensor-row setups; static vs dynamic weighing), use characteristics.config_level with axis describing the dimension.\"\n\nHere attributes vary by configuration (bore type × power supply) and per meter size. Each row groups multiple meter sizes with the same values.\n\nOptions:\n- matrix_tables[]: preserve the 4 tables as matrix tables with columns: meter_size, indicating_range_maximum, indicating_range_minimum, verification_scale_interval_maximum. Units: mm, m³, m³, m³.\n- config_level[]: For each attribute (indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max), axis = \"bore_type_and_power_supply\", with values per condition like \"reduced_bore, AC mains powered, DN40\" — but rows group sizes. E.g., \"reduced bore, AC mains: DN40, DN50, DN65\".\n\nHmm, could model each condition as combination: condition: \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered – DN40\" etc. But then rows with grouped sizes share values. I could list per meter size individually, expanding groups. That would be verbose but precise. For example Table 9 (reduced bore AC mains):\n- DN40: max 9999999, min 99999, VSI 0.0001\n- DN50: same\n- DN65: same\n- DN80: max 9999999, min 999999, VSI 0.0001\n- DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200: max 9999999, min 999999, VSI 0.001\n- DN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\n- DN300–DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nThat's a lot of entries. Alternatively keep conditions grouped as in the table: condition: \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered; meter size DN40, DN50, DN65\". That preserves fidelity to the source grouping.\n\nI think the cleanest approach:\n- matrix_tables[] with 4 tables (Table 9, 10, 11+continuation, 12). The page-12 first table is a continuation of Table 11 (Full bore – AC mains powered) — it has no title, follows Table 11, and lists DN350–DN1800. Note Table 11 full bore AC mains covers DN40–DN300 on page 11, and page 12 continues DN350–DN1800. So I should merge or represent as continuation. I'll create matrix_tables:\n  1. \"Table 9: Indicating device – Reduced bore – AC mains powered\"\n  2. \"Table 10: Indicating device – Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\"\n  3. \"Table 11: Indicating device – Full bore – AC mains powered\" (including continuation rows from page 12)\n  4. \"Table 12: Indicating device – Full bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\"\n\nColumns: meter_size, indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max. Column units: \"mm\", \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\".\n\nRows: meter_size values as arrays? The schema example shows rows as objects with scalar values. Meter size is a list like \"DN40, DN50, DN65\". I could keep as string \"DN40, DN50, DN65\" or array [\"DN40\",\"DN50\",\"DN65\"]. Array is nicer. The schema example in rules uses scalars, but rows are \"object per row\" so flexible. I'll use arrays for meter_size groups to preserve the grouping. Hmm, or expand per DN. Let me keep the grouped representation as arrays — it preserves source fidelity and is compact.\n\nAlso config_level: Should I populate it? The schema wants characteristics.config_level for attribute × config-axis matrix. Given the tables capture it fully in matrix_tables, I could add config_level entries with condition