ished models. Since this is a fragment (part 4 of 4), I only extract what's here. The instruction: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\"\n\nSo certificate.number = \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", revision... The number line says \"Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 14\". I'd set number to \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and could note revision 14. The schema doesn't have a revision field on certificate; revision_history has revisions. I could include revision \"14\" in revision_history (it is there as last row). Also page_total = 13 (from \"Page 11 of 13\"). member_state = \"The Netherlands\". project_number = \"4009997\". scheme: The number contains \"-A-\" which indicates scheme A per OIML-CS. Rule: scheme (A|B). \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — the \"A\" indicates scheme A. I'll set scheme: \"A\". Recommendation: R49 (water meters), edition 2013 (from \"R49/2013\"). The canonical id: \"R49\". edition: 2013. accuracy_classes: revision 6 mentions \"accuracy class 1\" but that's in history, not a definitive class list. I'd omit accuracy_classes or... The fragment doesn't state the classes directly except history mentions. I'll omit to avoid fabrication. Actually revision 6 says \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800\" — implies class 1 exists, and revision 3 mentions E2 and O class for flow transmitter. That's too fragmentary; omit accuracy_classes.\n\nissuing_authority: NMi — name \"NMi\" (NMi Certin probably, but fragment says \"NMi\"). address_lines not present. oiml_issuer_id: NL1? The number contains \"NL1\" which is the issuer ID (NMi Certin B.V. is NL1... actually NMi is issuer NL1? For NMi Certin, the OIML authority code is \"NL1\"? Hmm. In the cert number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", NL1 is likely the issuing authority identifier. I could set oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\". Is that fabrication? It's derived from the number structure. Reasonable. I'll include it in both certificate and issuing_authority. Hmm, risk: